<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Adaptive icon.

  THE FOREGROUND IS THE BARE MARK, NOT A TILE. Until 2026-09-02 this pointed at
  a foreground that was itself a finished icon - a paper-colored rounded square
  with the mark inside it and a margin around that - so the launcher was masking
  a tile that already had its own edge, and the mark ended up small in the
  middle of two nested shapes. The layer now carries only the three plates on
  transparency, which is what a foreground layer is for.

  THE SAFE ZONE IS CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED. A launcher crops this layer to the
  inner 66dp of 108dp. The mark's furthest vertex - the top plate's right tip,
  not the bounding-box corner, which the shear cuts away - sits 30.84dp from
  center against a 33dp safe radius: 2.16dp clear. site/icons/src/render.py
  asserts that on every run, so a change to the geometry fails loudly instead of
  quietly clipping on round-mask devices.

  MONOCHROME IS THE SAME PATHS, FLAT BLACK. Android takes the alpha and applies
  its own tint for themed icons, so the amber middle plate becomes the same
  value as the outer two. Three identical bars reads as deliberate; graying the
  middle plate to hint at amber would be the muddy version, and is why this
  layer is drawn from the silhouette rather than from the color artwork.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
